answer:
1. Static Data Member:
- Only one copy exists and is shared by all objects of the class.
- Initialized to 0 by default outside class.
- Lifetime is the entire program duration.

2. Static Member Function:
- Can access only static data members and call only static functions.
- Can be called using class name and scope resolution operator (::) without creating an object.

Example Program:
#include <iostream>
using namespace std;
class Item {
    static int count; // Static data member
public:
    Item() { count++; }
    static void showCount() { // Static member function
        cout << "Total Objects: " << count << endl;
    }
};
int Item::count = 0; // Definition outside class
int main() {
    Item a, b, c;
    Item::showCount(); // 3
    return 0;
}
